is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The failure is on router2’s Server Room interface (eth0) IP configuration. pc1 is configured as 1.0.0.5/30, which places it in the 1.0.0.4/30 subnet (usable hosts .5 and .6). Its gateway must be on-link in that /30. However, router2’s eth0 is currently set to 1.0.0.1 with a 255.0.0.0 (/8) mask (evidence: router2 ifconfig shows “inet 1.0.0.1 netmask 255.0.0.0”), and its routing table installs 1.0.0.0/8 as a connected network. This makes 1.0.0.1 off-subnet from pc1’s 1.0.0.4/30 view, so pc1 will not ARP or forward to that “gateway,” yielding “Network is unreachable” when pinging 1.0.0.1 (evidence: pc1 ping shows Network is unreachable; pc1 routes only 1.0.0.4/30). Conversely, router2 cannot ping pc1 either (100% loss) because pc1 can’t return traffic to an off-subnet 1.0.0.1.

Localization: router2 eth0 (Server Room/LAN_servers). Mechanism: subnet mask/address mismatch between router2 (1.0.0.1/8) and pc1 (1.0.0.5/30) breaks L3 adjacency. Additional change on router2: RIP is configured to advertise 1.0.0.0/8 (router rip: “network 1.0.0.0/8”), which is inconsistent with a /30 server LAN.

Root cause: During maintenance, router2’s eth0 was readdressed/widened from the intended /30 server subnet to 1.0.0.1/8 and RIP was set to advertise 1.0.0.0/8.

Corrective fix: Reconfigure router2 eth0 to the correct /30 addressing for LAN_servers (e.g., 1.0.0.6/30 as gateway for pc1=1.0.0.5/30) and update RIP to advertise only that /30 (or remove 1.0.0.0/8). After this, set/verify pc1’s default gateway to the corrected on-link address (1.0.0.6). === END ===